BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
DTSTART:20261203T223000Z
DTEND:20261203T233000Z
LOCATION:Wells Library Screening Room 048
GEO:39.171006;-86.5168
SUMMARY:You (probably) Haven't Seen This Before - Best Practices: Films f
 or Safe Living
DESCRIPTION:Dive deep into the archival collections of IU Libraries throu
 gh a screening series of rare and unique archival films that center tradi
 tionally underrepresented narratives and underrepresented motion-picture 
 practices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum of what is considered "
 cinema."  In an effort to keep audiences open-minded and with limited pre
 -conceptions of these rare works of celluloid\, descriptions of films to 
 be shown will not be shared prior to screenings. Join us based on announc
 ed themes and the expectation that the works you see will fall outside of
  Hollywood tropes\, outside of mainstream commercial expectations\, and o
 utside of conventional filmmaking practices. Trust us\, you (probably) ha
 ven't seen these films before!December Program: Best Practices: Films for
  Safe Living curated by Dave RodriguezProgram Description: Can a film sav
 e your life? Can it give you a design for living? These will certainly tr
 y.Curator Bio: Dave Rodriguez is a media artist\, musician\, and curator 
 from Miami\, FL\, currently based in Bloomington\, IN. His film and video
  work has screened in festivals and galleries across the Americas and Eur
 ope including the Ann Arbor Film Festival\, the Chicago Underground Film 
 Festival\, Mono No Aware\, Third Horizon\, and others. He has over 15 yea
 rs of professional experience working in film labs\, libraries\, archives
 \, and museum collections\, publishing in places like Found Footage Magaz
 ine\, the Journal of Academic Librarianship\, and most recently in the Re
 view of Japanese Culture and Society. In Bloomington he spends his time t
 aking care of many plants\, one old cat\, and supporting local organizati
 ons like Cicada Cinema and the Bloomington Synth Community.Please registe
 r below! Advance registration is strongly encouraged and deeply appreciat
 ed!
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Dive deep into the archival collections
  of IU Libraries through a screening series of rare and unique archival f
 ilms that center traditionally underrepresented narratives and underrepre
 sented motion-picture practices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum o
 f what is considered "cinema."</p><p>  In an effort to keep audiences ope
 n-minded and with limited pre-conceptions of these rare works of celluloi
 d\, descriptions of films to be shown will not be shared prior to screeni
 ngs. Join us based on announced themes and the expectation that the works
  you see will fall outside of Hollywood tropes\, outside of mainstream co
 mmercial expectations\, and outside of conventional filmmaking practices.
  Trust us\, you (probably) haven't seen these films before!</p><p><strong
 >December Program:</strong> Best Practices: Films for Safe Living curated
  by Dave Rodriguez</p><p><strong>Program Description:</strong> Can a film
  save your life? Can it give you a design for living? These will certainl
 y try.</p><p><strong>Curator Bio:</strong> Dave Rodriguez is a media arti
 st\, musician\, and curator from Miami\, FL\, currently based in Blooming
 ton\, IN. His film and video work has screened in festivals and galleries
  across the Americas and Europe including the Ann Arbor Film Festival\, t
 he Chicago Underground Film Festival\, Mono No Aware\, Third Horizon\, an
 d others. He has over 15 years of professional experience working in film
  labs\, libraries\, archives\, and museum collections\, publishing in pla
 ces like <i>Found Footage Magazine</i>\, the<i> Journal of Academic Libra
 rianship</i>\, and most recently in the <i>Review of Japanese Culture and
  Society</i>. In Bloomington he spends his time taking care of many plant
 s\, one old cat\, and supporting local organizations like Cicada Cinema a
 nd the Bloomington Synth Community.</p><p><strong>Please register below! 
 Advance registration is strongly encouraged and deeply appreciated!</stro
 ng></p><p><br /><br /></p>
